## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Replace the hosted dashboard Work tab's basic artifact list with a familiar folder-style hierarchy
derived from the established Idea, Map, PRM, and Campaign parent/child relationships. This is an
indented expandable tree inside the existing work table, not a node-and-arrow diagram. Each artifact
remains a row and retains the useful Document, Closure, Release, Outcome, and Reconciliation state.

Give every tree entry a small square command button. Clicking the button opens a compact,
state-aware menu whose entries use clear human descriptions such as **Continue this campaign** or
**Investigate the blocker**. Selecting an entry copies the exact corresponding `ts:` command to the
browser clipboard for the operator to paste into Codex. It does not execute the command, submit an
action to the hosted service, or mutate local Tool Shed state.

### Why It Matters

The current newest-first table makes the operator mentally reconstruct lineage from a Relationships
column and can make sibling Campaigns look sequential. A folder tree makes ownership, branching,
and current context visible immediately. The command menu then turns each visible artifact into a
safe starting point for local Codex work without requiring the operator to remember command syntax
or granting the hosted dashboard control over the local workspace.

This extends two completed directions without reopening them: `IDEA-0012` established the bounded
relationship-aware hosted Work inventory, and `IDEA-0022` established descriptive clipboard-copy
commands as a safe bridge from advisory hosted state to locally authoritative Tool Shed actions.

### Desired Outcome

The Work tab presents each reporting instance's current artifacts as one or more expandable,
folder-style trees. Roots and children are ordered predictably, indentation and chevrons expose the
parent/child structure, and the first column no longer requires a separate prose Relationships
column for ordinary lineage. Existing state columns remain available for row-level inspection.

Every displayed Idea, Map, PRM, and Campaign has a compact square command-menu button. Its menu is
generated from a controlled local allowlist using only the artifact type, stable visible ID,
lifecycle, current blocker or finding state, and applicable release posture. Menu labels and short
descriptions explain the effect in plain language; the underlying exact command may remain
secondary or hidden. Selecting an item copies the command, gives accessible confirmation, and
never executes it.

Menus include common continuation and inspection routes appropriate to the artifact. Blocked work
adds commands that inspect or mitigate the blocker and, where valid, unblock the Campaign. A current
actionable loop finding may offer its exact `ts: resolve loop <LOOP-id>` route. Commands use stable
public IDs and force Codex to re-read authoritative local state after paste; they never contain
credentials, local paths, state tokens, raw diagnostic text, or pre-authorized mutations.

### Candidate Interaction

```text
▾ MAP-0029  Enforced App Server Dispatch and Closure                         [>_]
  ▾ PRM-0041  Enforced App Server Dispatch and Closure                     [>_]
      CAMP-0165  Build Atomic Correlated App Server Dispatch                  [>_]
      CAMP-0166  Enforce and Qualify App Server Dispatch Closure              [>_]
```

Selecting `[>_]` on `CAMP-0166` could show descriptive choices such as:

- **Continue this campaign** — resume the exact Campaign from its current step.
- **Review current status** — explain progress, blockers, evidence, and the next safe action.
- **Investigate the blocker** — diagnose what prevents progress and recommend the smallest mitigation.
- **Return it to the queue** — clear a valid blocked state without starting execution.

Clicking a choice copies only its exact allowlisted `ts:` command and briefly confirms which action
was copied.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Local Tool Shed state remains authoritative; the hosted dashboard remains an advisory projection.
- The interface is a folder-style indented tree/table, not a graphical node-and-edge diagram.
- Preserve existing useful state dimensions rather than hiding them in the tree.
- Use reported stable relationships; do not infer hierarchy from titles, timestamps, or ID prefixes.
- Preserve reporting-instance boundaries and freshness; do not merge divergent instance trees.
- Command menus are built from a product-defined allowlist, controlled state, and stable public IDs.
- A click copies text to the clipboard only. It does not execute, enqueue, approve, or remotely
  deliver an action.
- Codex must resolve current project identity, state, authority, and safety after the command is pasted.
- Never copy credentials, filesystem paths, prompts, source text, transient tokens, raw diagnostics,
  shell commands, or unrestricted operator-authored text.
- History-only or resolved entries receive no mutation command. Read-only inspection may remain.
- Explicit non-goal: add a hosted-to-local action-intent channel, polling agent, or remote-control API.
- Explicit non-goal: duplicate lifecycle, blocker, reconciliation, PRM, Campaign, or release logic in
  the dashboard.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Folder-style tree table as the default Work view | Makes ancestry and sibling branches obvious while preserving aligned state columns | Requires deterministic root, child, expansion, and filter behavior | Selected direction |
| Replace the table with nested cards or a node graph | Visually emphasizes hierarchy | Loses scan-friendly column alignment and does not match the requested familiar folder-tree interaction | Rejected |
| Keep an optional flat List view | Preserves global sorting and column comparison for audits | Adds a small view-mode control and another presentation path to test | Candidate complement |
| One square command button per artifact | Keeps commands discoverable without widening every row | Needs accessible labeling, keyboard behavior, and compact popover placement | Selected direction |
| Descriptive menu labels with hidden exact commands | Lets operators choose by intent instead of memorizing syntax | Copy confirmation must make the selected action unambiguous | Selected direction |
| State-aware allowlisted commands | Offers continuation, review, blocker, mitigation, and release actions only when relevant | Requires one canonical mapping from artifact type/state to safe command templates | Selected direction |
| A uniform `ts: continue <artifact-id>` route | Makes every menu predictable and keeps copied text short | Would add a new canonical dispatcher that must resolve type and state deterministically | Open option |
| Existing type-specific commands only | Reuses established Tool Shed routes | Some Maps and PRMs lack one concise ID-targeted continuation command | Open option |

## Open Questions

- Should the flat List view remain available as a toggle, or should the tree table fully replace it?
- What is the exact canonical command allowlist for each Idea, Map, PRM, and Campaign lifecycle state?
- Should Tool Shed add a uniform `ts: continue <artifact-id>` route or expose only existing typed routes?
- When filters match a descendant but not its parent, should ancestors appear as dimmed context rows?
- What deterministic order should apply to roots and siblings: planning order, Campaign queue order,
  most recent descendant activity, or a controlled combination?
- Should trees initially expand all active branches and collapse completed branches, with the choice
  remembered only in the browser?
- Which blocked states support direct `unblock`, which require read-only diagnosis, and which can
  expose an exact current `ts: resolve loop <LOOP-id>` command?
- When a malformed or legacy item has more than one reported parent, should the UI show one canonical
  placement plus an **also linked to** reference, or surface it as a lineage finding?
- Should the copied command be available as optional secondary text in the menu for operator review,
  or remain hidden until the copy confirmation?

## Decisions

- Use a familiar folder-style tree with indentation and expand/collapse controls.
- Keep each artifact as a row with its existing operational state columns.
- Put one small square command-menu button on every displayed entry.
- Describe menu actions in plain language; the description does not need to expose the raw command.
- Clicking an action copies its exact command to the clipboard for manual paste into Codex.
- The dashboard does not execute or remotely deliver copied commands.
- Make menus specific to artifact type and current state.
- Blocked entries should offer applicable unblock or mitigation-oriented commands.
- Reuse the established local-authority and privacy boundary from prior dashboard work.

## Don't Forget

- Avoid repeating chain-level release badges on every ancestor when one tree-level rollup can explain it.
- Preserve row-specific lifecycle state so a completed Campaign awaiting Work5 is distinguishable from
  a currently working sibling.
- Keep essential menu labels and confirmation accessible to keyboard and assistive-technology users.
- Provide a clipboard fallback when the Clipboard API is unavailable.
- Prevent filtered results from becoming misleading orphan rows with no visible ancestry.
- Validate copied commands against current canonical Tool Shed syntax; never generate arbitrary text
  from titles or descriptions.
- Use concise confirmation such as **Continue campaign command copied** without implying execution.

